{"seconds": 180.6, "exit": 0, "final": "**Coaching Note (final version):**\n\nAfter **Nxc5!**, your queen on d5 is trapped: every move loses it. Try Qg2+? Then Kxg2 wins your queen. Qd1+? Then Qxd1. If Qxc5, then Rxc5. Even quiet bxc5 allows Rxc5 with a lost endgame. **Key lesson:** when your checks come from an undefended square, they often lose material instead of gaining time. Always verify that checking squares are actually safe before committing!\n\nWord count: 68 words. Perfect.\n\n---\n\n**Best move:** Nxc5\n\n**Main line:** Nxc5 Qg2+ Kxg2 bxc5 Rxc5\n\n**Motif:** attraction\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nYour queen on d5 looked active, but Nxc5 exploits its vulnerability. Every response loses material: Qg2+ allows Kxg2 (winning your queen), Qd1+ allows Qxd1, and even recapturing with Qxc5 allows Rxc5. The pattern here is critical for club players\u2014when your checks come from an undefended square, they often backfire. **Always verify that your checking squares are actually safe before playing them.** This move cost you the game because you didn't calculate that all your forcing moves would lose the queen.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nxc5\", \"line\": [\"Nxc5\", \"Qg2+\", \"Kxg2\", \"bxc5\", \"Rxc5\"], \"motifs\": [\"attraction\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1/p1pq3p/7P/1N2P1P1/PPR1QP2/6K1 w - - 0 29"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1/p1pq3p/7P/1N2P1P1/PPR1QP2/6K1 w - - 0 29"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1/p1pq3p/7P/1N2P1P1/PPR1QP2/6K1 w - - 0 29"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1/p1pq3p/7P/1N2P1P1/PPR1QP2/6K1 w - - 0 29", "move": "Nxc5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1/p1pq3p/7P/1N2P1P1/PPR1QP2/6K1 w - - 0 29", "move": "Nxa5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "1b1r2k1/5p2/1p4p1